const e=[{id:"sv-1-196",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt runs a binomial capability analysis on one quarter of **rejected roaming-activation requests** (long-term data):

\`\`\`
Binomial Process Capability Analysis: Rejected

Summary Stats (95.0% confidence)
%Defective:      0.62
Lower CI:        0.55
Upper CI:        0.70
Target:          0.00
PPM Def:         6210
Lower CI:        5480
Upper CI:        7010
Process Z:       2.50
Lower CI:        2.46
Upper CI:        2.54
\`\`\`

Using the conventional **1.5σ shift**, what **sigma level** should be reported for this process?`,options:["1.0σ","2.5σ","4.0σ","5.0σ"],answer:2,hint:"Minitab’s Process Z from long-term data is Z_LT; the reported (short-term) sigma level = Z_LT + 1.5.",steps:[{title:"Read the right number",body:"The decision number is **Process Z = 2.50**. It comes from a quarter of data, so it is a **long-term** Z ($Z_{LT}$)."},{title:"Apply the convention",body:"$$Z_{ST}=Z_{LT}+1.5=2.50+1.5=4.0$$"},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Reporting 2.5σ ignores the shift; 1.0σ subtracts it the wrong way; 5.0σ doubles Z as if the defects were split over two tails."}],final:"Sigma level = 2.50 + 1.5 = 4.0σ → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Subtracted the 1.5σ shift (2.50 − 1.5) instead of adding it to the long-term Z.","Reported Minitab’s long-term Process Z directly, ignoring the 1.5σ shift convention.","","Doubled the Z value as if it had to cover two tails; the shift convention simply adds 1.5."],trap:"Long-term data → add 1.5 to reach the short-term sigma level that is usually quoted.",bookRef:"Book pp. 223, 225"},{id:"sv-1-197",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The book uses the term **breakthrough improvement**. Which description matches it?",options:["Any improvement that reaches exactly 3.4 DPMO","An improvement of at or near **70%** over the baseline performance of the as-is process characteristic","A small, continuous improvement made every week by the process team (kaizen)","Any change approved by the Champion at the Improve tollgate"],answer:1,hint:"Breakthrough is defined against the baseline — a large step change, not a fixed sigma value.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary defines **breakthrough improvement** as a rate of improvement **at or near 70% over baseline** performance of the as-is process characteristic."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"It is measured **relative to the baseline** that Measure establishes — not by reaching a set number such as 3.4 DPMO."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Small weekly gains are kaizen-style incremental improvement, the opposite of breakthrough; a Champion’s sign-off is a governance step, not a size of improvement."}],final:"Breakthrough ≈ 70% improvement over baseline → answer **B**",whyWrong:["3.4 DPMO is the long-term defect rate of a 6σ process, not the definition of breakthrough improvement.","","This describes incremental (kaizen-style) improvement — the opposite of a breakthrough step change.","Tollgate approval is a governance event; it says nothing about the size of the improvement."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-198",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"At an operator, the executive committee now chooses improvement projects only from its strategic goals, reviews them monthly with data, and rewards managers for fixing processes rather than for fire-fighting. Which meaning of Six Sigma does this BEST illustrate?",options:["Six Sigma as a **metric** — 3.4 DPMO","Six Sigma as a **methodology** — the DMAIC roadmap","Six Sigma as a **statistical tool** — the control chart","Six Sigma as a **management philosophy** — a data-driven way of running the business"],answer:3,hint:"Metric = a number; methodology = DMAIC steps; philosophy = how leadership runs the business.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Six Sigma has three meanings: a **metric** (sigma level, DPMO), a **methodology** (DMAIC) and a **management philosophy / system** (leadership aligns projects with strategy and manages by data)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Selecting projects from strategy, reviewing with data and changing what is rewarded are **leadership behaviours** — the management-philosophy meaning."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Nothing in the stem quotes a defect rate (metric) or describes the steps of a project (methodology). The control chart is one tool, not a meaning of Six Sigma."}],final:"Leadership running the business by data = management philosophy → answer **D**",whyWrong:["No defect rate or sigma level is mentioned; the stem is about how leaders manage, not a measure.","DMAIC is how a single project is run; the stem describes how the whole business chooses and rewards work.","A control chart is one statistical tool, not one of the three meanings of Six Sigma.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.1"},{id:"sv-1-199",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Why is the sigma level of a process usually **lower** when it is calculated from a year of data than from one week of data?",options:["Long-term data contain more measurement error because more people collect them","A week of data always has more defects, so its sigma level is higher","Long-term variation has had the chance to include most of the sources of variation acting on the process, so it is wider than short-term variation","Short-term data are always taken from the best shift, so the difference is caused by bias"],answer:2,hint:"Short-term variation = few sources have acted; long-term variation = most sources have had the chance to act.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary defines **long-term variation** as variation that has had the opportunity to experience the majority of the variation effects, and **short-term variation** as variation that has **not** yet experienced all the sources."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A year of data includes shift changes, new staff, system releases and seasonal load, so the spread is wider, the distance to the nearest specification in σ units is smaller, and the sigma level falls. This is why the 1.5σ shift convention exists."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The drop is not caused by measurement error or by a biased sample choice; and a week of data does not necessarily contain more defects."}],final:"Long-term data capture more sources of variation → answer **C**",whyWrong:["More collectors may add some error, but the main reason is that more process sources of variation have acted over the year.","Reversed logic: more defects would give a lower sigma level, not a higher one — and a short period need not contain more defects.","","Sampling only the best shift would be a bias problem; the drop happens even with fair sampling because long-term variation is wider."],trap:"Short-term Z > long-term Z — the conventional gap is 1.5σ.",bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-200",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which quality pioneer is known for the **quality trilogy** (quality planning, quality control and quality improvement) and for applying the Pareto idea to quality as the **“vital few and useful many”**?",options:["Joseph M. Juran","Walter A. Shewhart","Kaoru Ishikawa","Philip B. Crosby"],answer:0,hint:"Link each pioneer to his signature idea: control chart, cause-and-effect diagram, zero defects, quality trilogy.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Juran** brought the Pareto principle into quality management (“vital few, trivial/useful many”) and described quality management as a **trilogy** of planning, control and improvement."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"**Shewhart** — control chart and the PDCA cycle (1920s). **Ishikawa** — cause-and-effect (fishbone) diagram and quality circles. **Crosby** — “zero defects” and “quality is free”."}],final:"Quality trilogy + vital few = Juran → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Shewhart is linked to the control chart (1924) and the Plan-Do-Check-Act cycle, not the trilogy.","Ishikawa is linked to the fishbone (cause-and-effect) diagram and quality circles.","Crosby is linked to “zero defects” and “quality is free”, not the Pareto idea or the trilogy."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-201",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"The **Plan-Do-Check-Act** cycle was made famous in Japan by W. Edwards Deming, who often called it by another pioneer’s name. Who first developed the cycle?",options:["Bill Smith","Taiichi Ohno","Jack Welch","Walter A. Shewhart"],answer:3,hint:"Deming called PDCA the “Shewhart cycle”.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The improvement cycle was developed by **Walter A. Shewhart** at Bell Laboratories; Deming taught it in Japan in the 1950s and called it the **Shewhart cycle** (later PDSA). Shewhart also developed the control chart from 1924."},{title:"Why it matters",body:"PDCA is a direct ancestor of the structured DMAIC cycle: both plan from data, test a change and check the result before standardising."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Bill Smith (Motorola) proposed Six Sigma in 1986; Ohno built the Toyota Production System; Welch made Six Sigma a GE-wide strategy in 1995."}],final:"PDCA = the Shewhart cycle → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Bill Smith proposed Six Sigma at Motorola in the 1980s — decades after PDCA.","Ohno is the architect of the Toyota Production System (Lean), not PDCA.","Jack Welch popularised Six Sigma at GE in the 1990s; he did not develop PDCA.",""],trap:"Deming popularised PDCA; Shewhart created it.",bookRef:"Book p. 524"},{id:"sv-1-202",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Six Sigma was created at Motorola, but it became widely known after **General Electric**, under Jack Welch, adopted it as a company-wide strategy in the mid-1990s. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Created at Motorola (1980s) → made famous at GE (1995 onwards).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Motorola developed Six Sigma in the mid-1980s and won the Baldrige award in 1988. **GE** adopted it across the whole company from **1995** under Jack Welch, and its published savings made Six Sigma famous."},{title:"Decide",body:"The statement matches the history, so it is **True**."}],final:"Motorola created it, GE popularised it → answer **A** (True)",whyWrong:["","The statement is correct: GE’s company-wide adoption from 1995 is what made Six Sigma widely known."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-203",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these events in **chronological order**:

- **P** General Electric adopts Six Sigma company-wide
- **Q** Shewhart develops the control chart at Bell Laboratories
- **R** Motorola launches Six Sigma
- **S** Deming teaches statistical quality control to Japanese industry`,options:["Q → S → R → P","S → Q → R → P","Q → R → S → P","Q → S → P → R"],answer:0,hint:"1920s → 1950s → 1980s → 1990s.",steps:[{title:"Date each event",body:"Q control chart: **1924**. S Deming in Japan: **1950** onwards. R Motorola Six Sigma: **1986–87**. P GE company-wide: **1995**."},{title:"Order them",body:"Q → S → R → P."}],final:"Shewhart → Deming → Motorola → GE → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Deming taught in Japan after Shewhart had already developed the control chart; Deming built on Shewhart’s work.","Motorola’s Six Sigma (1980s) came decades after Deming’s work in Japan (1950s).","GE adopted Six Sigma after Motorola had created it, not before."],bookRef:"Book p. 524"},{id:"sv-1-204",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A project that cut bill-dispute handling time is presented at its **Control** tollgate. The new process is running, a control chart is in place and savings are shown. The Champion still will not close the project. Which item is MOST likely missing?",options:["A second Pareto chart of dispute reasons","Evidence that the control plan has been **handed over to and accepted by the process owner**","A new Gage R&R study on the original measurement system","A revised SIPOC for a different process"],answer:1,hint:"Control closes when the gain is sustainable without the project team — ownership moves to the process owner.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Control tollgate checklist asks: “Has the Control Plan been handed off to the process owner?” and “Do the Champion, the Belt and Finance all agree this project is complete?”"},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The process, the chart and the savings exist, but nothing shows that the **process owner** has accepted the control plan and response plan. Without that hand-over, the gain depends on the project team."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A Pareto chart and a SIPOC are Define-phase tools; a Gage R&R on the old system belongs to Measure."}],final:"Missing: control plan accepted by the process owner → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A Pareto chart is a Define/Measure focusing tool; it is not a Control deliverable.","","MSA belongs to Measure; repeating it on the old system does not show the gain is sustained.","A SIPOC of another process is out of scope and is a Define-phase tool."],bookRef:"Book pp. 577–578"},{id:"sv-1-205",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"After Define, a team says: *“We all know the cause is the old CRM screen, so let’s skip Measure and Analyze and go straight to Improve.”* Which deliverable would be missing if they did this?",options:["The project charter and problem statement","The SIPOC and high-level process map","A validated measurement system, a baseline and **verified** root causes","A control plan with response plans"],answer:2,hint:"Measure → trusted data and a baseline; Analyze → proven X’s.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Measure delivers a **validated measurement system** and a **baseline** (capability / sigma level); Analyze delivers **statistically verified** critical X’s."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Skipping both leaves the team acting on an opinion, with no baseline to prove later that anything improved."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The charter and SIPOC are Define deliverables that already exist; the control plan comes after Improve."}],final:"Missing: MSA, baseline and verified X’s → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The charter is a Define deliverable — already completed before this decision.","The SIPOC and macro map are Define deliverables — already completed.","","The control plan is produced in Control, after Improve; it is not what skipping Measure and Analyze removes."],trap:"“We already know the cause” is the classic reason to skip Analyze — and the classic reason projects fail.",bookRef:"Book pp. 229–231"},{id:"sv-1-206",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is the main purpose of a **tollgate review** at the end of each DMAIC phase?",options:["To check that the phase deliverables are complete and decide whether the project should continue, change or stop","To calculate the final financial savings of the project","To train new Green Belts in the tools of the next phase","To hand the improved process to the process owner"],answer:0,hint:"Tollgate = a go / no-go gate with the Champion.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"At each tollgate the Champion (with the Belt) reviews the phase checklist — are the deliverables complete and sound? — and decides **go, redirect or stop**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Final savings are confirmed at closure with Finance; training is separate; hand-over to the process owner happens only in Control."}],final:"Tollgate = check deliverables + go / no-go decision → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Final savings are validated with Finance at closure, not at every phase tollgate.","Tollgates are project governance reviews, not training sessions.","Hand-over to the process owner happens once, in Control — not at every tollgate."],bookRef:"Book pp. 230–231"},{id:"sv-1-207",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"All of the following are typical deliverables of the **Measure** phase EXCEPT:",options:["A detailed (Level 3) process map","A measurement system analysis of the main output","A baseline capability or sigma level","A piloted solution with before-and-after results"],answer:3,hint:"Measure = map, measurement system, baseline, potential X’s. Piloting belongs to Improve.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Measure delivers a detailed process map, a validated measurement system, a **baseline** capability and a prioritised list of **potential** X’s."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"A **pilot** with before-and-after results tests a solution — that is an **Improve** deliverable."}],final:"Pilot results belong to Improve → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The detailed process map is built in Measure — it is a Measure deliverable.","MSA is a core Measure deliverable.","The baseline is a core Measure deliverable.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 230–231"},{id:"sv-1-208",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"In Measure, the baseline shows the real problem is twice as large as the charter estimate, and one region is out of scope. What should the Black Belt do with the **project charter**?",options:["Leave it unchanged, because a signed charter cannot be altered","Start a completely new project and close this one as failed","Ignore the new data until the Control phase","Update the charter (problem, scope, goal) and get the Champion to re-approve it"],answer:3,hint:"The charter is a living document, reviewed at every tollgate.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The charter is a **living document**. As data replace estimates, the problem statement, scope and goal are revised and the Champion re-approves them."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"New baseline figures and a scope change are exactly what should flow back into the charter at the Measure tollgate."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Freezing the charter keeps a wrong problem statement; restarting the project wastes the work done; ignoring data contradicts a data-driven method."}],final:"Update the charter and re-approve it → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A charter is not frozen — it is updated as data replace estimates.","The project is still valid; the charter only needs updating, not a new project.","Ignoring new data contradicts the data-driven approach and leaves the goal wrong for three phases.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 230–231"},{id:"sv-1-209",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt shows this output at a tollgate:

\`\`\`
Process Capability Report for Restore time (h)

Process Data
LSL          *
Target       *
USL          4
Sample Mean  2.91
Sample N     612

Overall Capability
Ppk     0.61
Z.Bench 1.83
Observed PPM > USL   36,000
\`\`\`

The data are last quarter’s incidents, **before any change** was made. At which tollgate does this output normally belong, and as what?`,options:["Define — as the project goal","Analyze — as proof of the root cause","Measure — as the baseline performance","Control — as evidence that the gain is sustained"],answer:2,hint:"Data taken before any change = baseline.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **baseline** — current capability, sigma level or defect rate of the Y before any change — is a **Measure** deliverable."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The report uses last quarter’s data before any change, so it describes the as-is performance (Ppk 0.61, 3.6% of incidents beyond the 4-hour USL)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A goal is a target, not current capability; capability does not prove a cause; and Control would show after-change data."}],final:"Before-change capability = Measure baseline → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The goal in the charter is a target value; this report shows current performance.","A capability report describes the Y; it does not test whether an X causes it.","","Control evidence would use data **after** the change; these are before-change data."],bookRef:"Book pp. 230–231"},{id:"sv-1-210",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A care team models **repeat-call rate** (%) per agent:

\`\`\`
Regression Analysis: RepeatRate versus Tenure, KBUse, CallLoad

Coefficients
Term        Coef   SE Coef  T-Value  P-Value
Constant   14.80     2.10     7.05    0.000
Tenure     -0.09     0.11    -0.82    0.412
KBUse      -0.21     0.04    -5.25    0.000
CallLoad    0.06     0.03     2.21    0.031
\`\`\`

At **α = 0.05**, which inputs should the team treat as **critical X’s** for now?`,options:["KBUse and CallLoad","Tenure only","All three, because each has a coefficient","KBUse only, because it has the largest T-value"],answer:0,hint:"A term is statistically significant when its P-Value ≤ α.",steps:[{title:"Read the decision numbers",body:"P-Values: Tenure 0.412, KBUse 0.000, CallLoad 0.031."},{title:"Apply the rule",body:"KBUse (0.000) and CallLoad (0.031) are ≤ 0.05 → significant. Tenure (0.412) > 0.05 → fail to reject H₀; no evidence it affects the repeat-call rate in these data."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Every term has a coefficient, so having one proves nothing. CallLoad is also significant even though its T-value is smaller than KBUse’s."}],final:"p ≤ 0.05 for KBUse and CallLoad → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Reversed the rule: Tenure has the only p-value above 0.05, so it is the one input **not** shown to matter.","Every term in a model has a coefficient; significance is judged on the P-Value, not on its presence.","CallLoad (p = 0.031) is also significant; ranking by T-value does not remove a significant X."],bookRef:"Book pp. 208, 590"},{id:"sv-1-211",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`To check whether **shift** is an X for ticket resolution time, a Black Belt runs:

\`\`\`
One-way ANOVA: ResTime versus Shift

Source  DF  Adj SS  Adj MS  F-Value  P-Value
Shift    2   412.6   206.3     6.42    0.003
Error   87  2795.4    32.1
Total   89  3208.0
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct at **α = 0.05**?`,options:["Fail to reject H₀ — shift is not an X","Accept H₀ — the three shifts have equal mean resolution times","Reject H₀ — at least one shift’s mean resolution time differs, so shift is a candidate X","Reject H₀ — all three shifts have different mean resolution times"],answer:2,hint:"p ≤ α → reject H₀; ANOVA tells you that at least one mean differs, not which ones.",steps:[{title:"Read the decision number",body:"P-Value = 0.003."},{title:"Decide",body:"0.003 ≤ 0.05 → **reject H₀**. At least one shift mean differs, so shift belongs in Y = f(X) as an X worth investigating."},{title:"Word it carefully",body:"ANOVA does not say that **all** means differ — a comparison such as Tukey is needed for that. And when p > α the correct wording is “fail to reject”, never “accept”."}],final:"p = 0.003 < 0.05 → reject H₀; shift is a candidate X → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Reversed the p-value rule: 0.003 is below α, so H₀ is rejected.","Wrong decision and wrong wording — H₀ is rejected here, and H₀ is never “accepted”.","","Overstates the result: ANOVA shows at least one mean differs, not that every pair differs."],trap:"ANOVA significant ≠ “all means differ”.",bookRef:"Book p. 208"},{id:"sv-1-212",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team regresses **first-time-fix rate** on technician experience:

\`\`\`
Regression Analysis: FTF versus Experience

Term         Coef  SE Coef  T-Value  P-Value
Constant    68.40     2.90    23.59    0.000
Experience   0.92     0.29     3.17    0.002

S = 7.84   R-sq = 21.4%   R-sq(adj) = 20.6%
\`\`\`

What does this tell the team about **Y = f(X)**?`,options:["Experience is not an X because R-sq is below 50%","Experience explains almost all of the variation in FTF, so the project can move to Improve","The model is invalid because the constant is significant","Experience is a significant X, but it explains only about a fifth of the variation, so other important X’s are still missing"],answer:3,hint:"The P-Value says whether an X matters; R-sq says how much of the Y’s variation the model explains.",steps:[{title:"Read both numbers",body:"Experience P-Value = 0.002 (significant). R-sq = 21.4%."},{title:"Interpret",body:"Experience **is** an X, but about 79% of the variation in FTF is still unexplained. In Y = f(X₁, X₂, …, Xₙ) the other X’s (parts availability, job type, diagnostics…) must still be found."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A low R-sq does not cancel a significant p-value; and a significant constant is normal — it is just the intercept."}],final:"Significant X, small share of variation → more X’s needed → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Confused “how much is explained” with “does it matter”: p = 0.002 shows Experience is a real X.","Read R-sq the wrong way: 21.4% is a small share, not almost all.","The intercept’s p-value has nothing to do with model validity; it is usually significant.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 208, 590"},{id:"sv-1-213",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"For a fibre-splicing process the team lists: splice-machine setting, technician training level, **outdoor temperature**, and the work instruction used. Which of these is a **noise (N)** input?",options:["Splice-machine setting","Technician training level","Outdoor temperature","The work instruction used"],answer:2,hint:"Controllable = can be set while the process runs; noise = affects Y but cannot be (or is not) controlled.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Inputs are classified as **controllable (C)** — adjustable while the process runs — or **noise (N)** — inputs that affect the output but are not controlled, such as weather. SOP inputs are those governed by procedures."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The team cannot set the outdoor temperature at a street cabinet, yet it can affect splice loss: it is **noise**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The machine setting is controllable; training level is managed by the business; the work instruction is an SOP input."}],final:"Outdoor temperature = noise → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A machine setting can be adjusted while the process runs — a controllable input.","Training level is decided and managed by the business — controllable, not noise.","","A work instruction is a procedure-governed (SOP) input."],bookRef:"Book pp. 113–114"},{id:"sv-1-214",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In **Y = f(X)**, the X’s are also called:",options:["Independent variables, inputs or causes","Dependent variables, outputs or effects","Specifications or customer requirements","Control limits"],answer:0,hint:"Y = output / effect / dependent. X = input / cause / independent.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Y is the **dependent** variable — the output or effect. The X’s are the **independent** variables — the inputs or causes that drive Y."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"“Dependent, output, effect” describes Y. Specifications come from the customer; control limits come from the process — neither is an X."}],final:"X = independent variable / input / cause → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","These terms describe Y, the output — the reverse of X.","Specifications are customer requirements placed on Y, not inputs.","Control limits are the voice of the process on a chart, not inputs."],bookRef:"Book p. 590"},{id:"sv-1-215",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A Black Belt has proven that **order-entry accuracy (X)** drives **activation lead time (Y)**. To control activation lead time in future, what should the team monitor and control **first**?",options:["Only the monthly average activation lead time","Order-entry accuracy, with a reaction plan, because it changes before the lead time does","Customer complaints about late activation","Nothing — once proven, an X needs no further control"],answer:1,hint:"Control the X’s and the Y follows; watching only Y reacts after the damage is done.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Y = f(X): the output is a result of its inputs. Controlling a **critical X** is the way to control Y — an X is a **leading** signal, while Y is a **lagging** one."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"If order-entry accuracy slips, longer lead times will follow. Monitoring that X with a reaction plan lets the team act before customers feel it."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Watching only Y or complaints finds problems late; and an uncontrolled critical X will drift back."}],final:"Monitor and control the critical X → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Monitoring only Y is lagging: by the time the average moves, many customers are already affected.","","Complaints are the latest possible signal — the opposite of controlling the cause.","A critical X must be controlled, by definition; left alone it will drift and Y will return to its old level."],bookRef:"Book p. 114"},{id:"sv-1-216",phase:1,topic:"1.1.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which definition of the **Voice of the Customer (VOC)** is the best fit?",options:["The needs of the business and its key stakeholders, such as profit and cost targets","The expressed and non-expressed needs, wants and desires of the recipients of a process output","The performance and capability of a process, shown by its control limits","The ideas and concerns of the people who work in the process"],answer:1,hint:"VOC = customers; VOB = business; VOP = process; VOE = employees.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary defines VOC as the **expressed and non-expressed** needs, wants and desires of the recipients of a process output."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Business needs = **VOB**; process performance and control limits = **VOP**; the views of the people who do the work = **VOE**."}],final:"VOC = customers’ expressed and unexpressed needs → answer **B**",whyWrong:["This is the Voice of the Business (VOB).","","This is the Voice of the Process (VOP).","This is the Voice of the Employee (VOE)."],trap:"“Non-expressed” needs count too — basic Kano needs are rarely spoken.",bookRef:"Book p. 590"},{id:"sv-1-217",phase:1,topic:"1.1.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A Black Belt already has a year of complaint logs for an enterprise SD-WAN service. She now wants VOC from customers who **have not complained** about needs they do not yet voice. Which source should she add?",options:["More detailed coding of the complaint log","Warranty and credit-note records","Calls to the technical support line","Structured interviews or site visits with a sample of enterprise customers"],answer:3,hint:"Reactive VOC arrives on the customer’s initiative; proactive VOC is collected on the company’s initiative.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Reactive** sources come to the company when the customer chooses (complaints, support calls, credits). **Proactive** sources are gathered by the company (surveys, interviews, focus groups, site visits)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Silent customers and unvoiced needs are only reached **proactively** — interviews and site visits let the team ask and observe."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Complaint logs, credits and support calls all come only from customers who chose to contact the company."}],final:"Proactive interviews / site visits → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Re-coding complaints still hears only customers who complained — reactive VOC.","Warranty and credit records are reactive: they exist only after something went wrong.","Support calls are customer-initiated — reactive VOC.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 112"},{id:"sv-1-218",phase:1,topic:"1.1.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Business customers ask for **same-day on-site repair, 7 days a week**. Finance requires that field-service cost **does not rise**. How should the Black Belt treat these two voices in Define?",options:["Follow the VOC only, because the customer is always the first priority","Follow the VOB only, because the sponsor pays for the project","Treat both as requirements: translate the VOC into CTQs and keep the VOB as a project constraint or secondary metric","Drop both and wait for the Voice of the Process from Measure"],answer:2,hint:"A good project balances VOC (CTQs) with VOB (business requirements) — neither is ignored.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"VOC tells the team what customers need; VOB tells it what the business needs (cost, margin, risk). Both feed the charter: VOC becomes the **CTQ / primary metric**, VOB a **constraint or secondary metric**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The project could aim at faster repair (CTQ) **without** extra field cost (secondary metric) — for example by better remote diagnosis or parts placement."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Ignoring either voice gives a solution that fails the customer or the business. VOP shows what the process does, not what is required."}],final:"VOC → CTQ, VOB → constraint / secondary metric → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A solution that ignores the business case will not be funded or sustained.","A cheaper process that still fails the customer’s CTQ does not solve the problem.","","VOP describes current performance; it cannot tell the team what customers or the business require."],bookRef:"Book p. 590"},{id:"sv-1-219",phase:1,topic:"1.1.5",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"The **Voice of the Process (VOP)** describes what the customer wants from the process, so it is used to set the specification limits. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Specification limits = voice of the customer; control limits = voice of the process.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **VOP** is the performance and capability of the process — what it actually delivers, shown by its control limits. **Specification limits** come from the **VOC**."},{title:"Decide",body:"The statement mixes the two voices, so it is **False**."}],final:"VOP = what the process does; specs = VOC → answer **B** (False)",whyWrong:["VOP shows what the process does (control limits). What the customer wants (specification limits) is the VOC.",""],trap:"Never draw specification limits on a control chart as if they were control limits.",bookRef:"Book pp. 525, 590"},{id:"sv-1-220",phase:1,topic:"1.1.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team collects **400** coded VOC comments about fixed broadband:

\`\`\`
Tally for Discrete Variables: Source

Source                  Count  Percent
Complaint log             182    45.50
Care-line calls            96    24.00
Social-media mentions      42    10.50
Relationship survey        54    13.50
Customer interviews        26     6.50
N=                        400
\`\`\`

What percentage of the comments come from **reactive** sources?`,options:["20.0%","45.5%","69.5%","80.0%"],answer:3,hint:"Reactive = the customer chose to contact or talk about the company; proactive = the company asked.",steps:[{title:"Classify the sources",body:"Reactive (customer-initiated): complaint log, care-line calls, social-media mentions. Proactive (company-initiated): survey, interviews."},{title:"Add the reactive percentages",body:"$$45.5+24.0+10.5=80.0\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Four-fifths of this VOC comes from customers who chose to speak up, so the quieter majority is under-represented — a reason to add proactive sources."}],final:"Reactive share = 80.0% → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This is the proactive share (survey + interviews = 13.5 + 6.5).","Counted only the complaint log; care calls and social-media mentions are also customer-initiated.","Left out social-media mentions (45.5 + 24.0), which are also unprompted, customer-initiated comments.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 112"},{id:"sv-1-221",phase:1,topic:"1.1.6",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Before launching Lean Six Sigma, an operator forms a **cross-functional executive team** to define and document deployment practices, methods and operating policies. In the book’s terms, what is this group?",options:["A project team","The Implementation Team","A Master Black Belt council","The Finance Representatives"],answer:1,hint:"Look for the role whose charter is the deployment itself, not one project.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary defines the **Implementation Team** as a cross-functional executive team representing various areas of the company, whose charter is to drive Six Sigma implementation by defining and documenting practices, methods and operating policies."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A project team works on one project; Master Black Belts are technical experts and coaches; Finance Representatives validate savings."}],final:"Executive team that drives deployment = Implementation Team → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A project team solves one problem under a Belt; it does not set deployment policy.","","Master Black Belts teach, mentor and give technical support — they are not the executive deployment team.","Finance Representatives give an independent evaluation of hard and soft savings."],bookRef:"Book p. 586"},{id:"sv-1-222",phase:1,topic:"1.1.6",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Which project is the BEST fit for a **Green Belt**?",options:["Redesigning the end-to-end order-to-cash process across five countries","Leading the company’s Six Sigma deployment and training plan","Reducing re-keying errors in her own team’s order-entry step, part time, with support from a Black Belt","Validating the hard savings claimed by all projects this year"],answer:2,hint:"Green Belt = part-time, smaller-scoped projects in their own area, and support for Black Belt projects.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary describes a Black Belt as a **part-time** role that applies Six Sigma to their **local area**, doing **smaller-scoped** projects and supporting Black Belt projects."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Re-keying errors in her own team’s step is local, focused and manageable part time."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A five-country redesign is Black Belt scope; deployment leadership belongs to the Six Sigma Leader or Implementation Team; savings validation is the Finance Representative’s job."}],final:"Local, smaller-scoped, part-time project → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A cross-country end-to-end redesign is large and complex — Black Belt scope.","Leading deployment is the Six Sigma Leader / Implementation Team role.","","Independent savings validation is the Finance Representative’s role."],bookRef:"Book p. 586"},{id:"sv-1-223",phase:1,topic:"1.1.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which role is described as the **technical go-to expert** who teaches and mentors other Belts and supports Champions?",options:["Master Black Belt","Process Owner","Champion","Process Member"],answer:0,hint:"Training beyond Black Belt + teaching and mentoring Belts.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **Master Black Belt** has training beyond Black Belt, is the technical go-to expert, teaches and mentors other Belts and their projects, and supports Champions."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The Process Owner owns process performance; the Champion sponsors and removes barriers; a Process Member does the work in the process."}],final:"Technical expert and mentor = MBB → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The Process Owner is responsible for process performance and resources, not for coaching Belts.","The Champion selects, sponsors and supports projects but is not the statistical expert.","A Process Member performs activities in the process."],bookRef:"Book p. 586"},{id:"sv-1-224",phase:1,topic:"1.1.6",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are normal responsibilities of a **Green Belt** EXCEPT:",options:["Leading a smaller-scoped DMAIC project in their own area","Collecting and analysing data with basic Six Sigma tools","Approving the project’s savings independently on behalf of Finance","Supporting a Black Belt on a larger project"],answer:2,hint:"Savings must be validated by someone independent of the project.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Black Belt leads smaller projects, uses the DMAIC tools and supports Black Belt projects."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"Savings need an **independent** check. That is the **Finance Representative’s** job — a project leader cannot independently approve their own savings."}],final:"Independent savings approval is Finance’s role → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Leading smaller local projects is the core Green Belt role.","Using the DMAIC tools on data is exactly what Green Belts are trained for.","","Supporting Black Belt projects is part of the Black Belt definition."],bookRef:"Book pp. 585–586"},{id:"sv-1-225",phase:1,topic:"1.1.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"How does the book describe a **Black Belt**?",options:["A part-time role that applies Six Sigma only to its own local area","An executive who owns the business roadmap and is accountable for project results","A team member who performs activities within the process","A full-time Six Sigma team leader, with about four weeks of training, who solves problems under the direction of a Champion"],answer:3,hint:"Black Belt ≈ 4 weeks of training, full time; Green Belt ≈ 2 weeks, part time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary: a Black Belt receives about **four weeks** of training and is a **full-time** Six Sigma team leader solving problems under the direction of a **Champion**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Part-time local work = Green Belt; accountable executive = Champion; performing activities = Process Member."}],final:"Full-time leader under a Champion → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This describes a Black Belt (part time, local area, about two weeks of training).","This describes a Champion, who is accountable for project results and the roadmap.","This describes a Process Member.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-226",phase:1,topic:"1.1.6",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A billing clerk applies payments and corrects account balances every day. She joins the project team to help map the process. In the book’s terms, what is her role in the process?",options:["Process Owner","Champion","Process Member","Finance Representative"],answer:2,hint:"Who does the work vs who is accountable for the process.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **Process Member** performs activities within a process to deliver its output. The **Process Owner** is responsible for process performance and resources and is accountable for implementing solutions."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"She does the daily work but is not accountable for the process’s performance — a Process Member, and a valuable team member because she knows how the work is really done."}],final:"Performs the activities = Process Member → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The Process Owner is accountable for process performance and resources — usually her manager, not the clerk.","A Champion sponsors and supports the project at management level.","","A Finance Representative validates savings; this clerk is not doing that."],bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-227",phase:1,topic:"1.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which tool shows a process as a single box, with **inputs as arrows going into** the box and **outputs as arrows coming out** of it?",options:["Input-Process-Output (IPO) diagram","Swim-lane (deployment) map","Value stream map","Cause-and-effect diagram"],answer:0,hint:"The simplest picture of “a process transforms inputs into outputs”.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary defines the **IPO diagram** as a visual representation of a process where inputs are arrows into a box (the process) and outputs are arrows coming out of the box."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A swim-lane map shows who does each step; a value stream map adds queues, cycle times and uptime; a cause-and-effect diagram lists possible causes of one effect."}],final:"Inputs → box → outputs = IPO diagram → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A swim-lane map splits the steps into lanes by person or department; it is not a single box.","A value stream map shows many steps with queue sizes and time data.","A fishbone shows possible causes of an effect, not inputs and outputs of a process box."],bookRef:"Book p. 586"},{id:"sv-1-228",phase:1,topic:"1.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A swim-lane map of business-fibre orders has lanes for Sales, Credit, Network Planning and Field Operations. Where on this map should the team look **first** for queues and bottlenecks?",options:["At the start and end ellipses","At the points where work crosses from one lane to another","Inside the longest activity boxes, because long names mean long tasks","At the legend that explains the symbols"],answer:1,hint:"Each lane crossing is a supplier–customer hand-off.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book notes that each time work moves across a swim lane there is a **supplier–customer interaction**, and this is usually where **bottlenecks and queues form**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Hand-offs such as Sales → Credit or Planning → Field are where orders wait in inboxes, lack information or bounce back."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Ellipses only mark start and end; the length of a box label says nothing about time."}],final:"Look at the lane-crossing hand-offs → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Ellipses only mark where the process starts and ends.","","The length of a step’s description is not evidence of delay; time data would be needed.","The legend is not part of the process flow."],bookRef:"Book p. 103"},{id:"sv-1-229",phase:1,topic:"1.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"For a complaint-handling process, the team wants one map that shows each step’s **cycle time**, **uptime**, **available hours** and the **size of the work queue** between steps. Which type of map should they draw?",options:["Linear flow map","Value stream map","SIPOC","Swim-lane map"],answer:1,hint:"Only one map type carries queue and time data for every step.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **value stream map** is a specialised map that shows numerous performance metrics linked to the speed of the process: queue or inventory sizes, C/T, uptime, hours and breaks."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A linear flow map shows only the sequence; a SIPOC is a high-level scoping tool; a swim-lane map shows who does what."}],final:"Queues + C/T + uptime = value stream map → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A linear flow map shows the order of steps but not queue sizes or time parameters.","","A SIPOC lists suppliers, inputs, high-level steps, outputs and customers — no step timings.","A swim-lane map shows responsibility and hand-offs, not queue sizes and uptime."],bookRef:"Book p. 105"},{id:"sv-1-230",phase:1,topic:"1.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which content belongs on a **Level 3 (micro)** process map but **not** on a Level 1 (macro) map?",options:["Step-level inputs and outputs with their specifications, the C/N classification of inputs, and estimates of DPU, yield and RTY","A few major steps that each combine several related activities","Only the process name and its start and end points","Only the suppliers and customers of the whole process"],answer:0,hint:"Level 1 = management view; Level 2 = worker view; Level 3 = improvement view with data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **Level 3** map is the improvement view: it shows all flows, set points, SOPs, inputs and outputs with their specifications, whether inputs are controllable or noise, and usually estimates of **DPU, yield, RTY** and value / non-value add."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Combining activities into a few major steps is the Level 1 description; name, start and end points are the first step of building any map; suppliers and customers are SIPOC content."}],final:"Step-level specs, C/N and yield data = Level 3 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Combining activities into a few major steps describes the Level 1 macro map.","The name and boundaries are needed for every map; they are not Level 3 content.","Suppliers and customers of the whole process are SIPOC content."],bookRef:"Book p. 116"},{id:"sv-1-231",phase:1,topic:"1.2.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are characteristics of a **Level 1 (macro)** process map EXCEPT:",options:["It combines related activities into one major processing step","It shows every decision point and rework loop in the process","It illustrates where the process fits into the big picture","It has minimal detail"],answer:1,hint:"Level 1 = big picture, major steps only.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists Level 1 characteristics: combines related activities into one major step, shows where the process fits into the big picture, has minimal detail and shows only major steps."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"Every decision point and rework loop is **detail** — it belongs on the Level 2 or Level 3 map."}],final:"Decision points and rework loops are not Level 1 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Combining related activities into one major step is a listed Level 1 characteristic.","","Showing how the process fits into the big picture is a listed Level 1 characteristic.","Minimal detail is a listed Level 1 characteristic."],bookRef:"Book p. 102"},{id:"sv-1-232",phase:1,topic:"1.2.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"In the standard process-mapping symbols, a **diamond** marks a decision point, and only two paths leave it: **Yes** and **No**. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Rectangle = activity; diamond = decision; ellipse = start/end; parallelogram = data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: “A DIAMOND signifies a decision point. Only two paths emerge from a decision point: No and Yes.”"},{title:"Decide",body:"The statement matches the standard symbol set, so it is **True**."}],final:"Diamond = Yes/No decision → answer **A** (True)",whyWrong:["","The statement matches the book’s standard symbols: a diamond is a decision point with only Yes and No paths."],bookRef:"Book p. 101"},{id:"sv-1-233",phase:1,topic:"1.2.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt is mapping a number-porting process. In which order should she complete these activities?

- **P** Draw the Level 3 micro map with step data such as DPU and yield
- **Q** Name the process and agree its start and end points
- **R** Draw the Level 2 map from the worker’s point of view
- **S** Draw the Level 1 macro map`,options:["S → Q → R → P","Q → S → R → P","Q → R → S → P","Q → S → P → R"],answer:1,hint:"Define the process first, then map from macro to micro.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Before drawing, the book’s Level 1 worksheet names the process and identifies the beginning and ending steps. The maps are then used **in order from macro to micro**, like a world map → country map → city map."},{title:"Order them",body:"Q (name and boundaries) → S (Level 1) → R (Level 2) → P (Level 3)."}],final:"Q → S → R → P → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Drawing the macro map before the name and boundaries are agreed means the map has no agreed scope.","","Swapped Level 1 and Level 2: the macro map comes before the worker-level map.","Swapped Level 2 and Level 3: the detailed micro map is the last one, built on the Level 2 map."],bookRef:"Book pp. 102, 108"},{id:"sv-1-234",phase:1,topic:"1.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"In a SIPOC for **“Activate enterprise leased line”**, the network-inventory database provides a free port number for each new circuit. How should these two items be placed in the SIPOC?",options:["Inventory database = Supplier; free port number = Input","Inventory database = Input; free port number = Supplier","Inventory database = Process step; free port number = Output","Inventory database = Customer; free port number = Output"],answer:0,hint:"A supplier is whoever or whatever provides an input (resources or information).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary defines a **supplier** as an individual or entity responsible for providing an input to a process in the form of resources or information. The **input** is the resource or information itself."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The database (a system) supplies information; the free port number is the information consumed by the activation process."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The database does not receive the output (not a customer) and is not an activity of this process; the port number is used by the process, not produced by it."}],final:"Database = Supplier, port number = Input → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Reversed the two: the source is the supplier and the thing provided is the input.","The database is not an activity of the process, and the port number is consumed, not produced.","The database does not receive the process output, and the port number is an input, not an output."],bookRef:"Book p. 589"},{id:"sv-1-235",phase:1,topic:"1.2.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`A Black Belt drafts this SIPOC for mobile number porting:

| Suppliers | Inputs | Process | Outputs | Customers |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Customer; Retail shop; Donor operator | Port request; ID document; Donor approval | 1 Open CRM screen → 2 Search customer → 3 Scan ID → 4 Check ID expiry → 5 Enter number → 6 Select donor → 7 Send request → 8 Wait for reply → 9 Check reply code → 10 Book port slot → 11 Send SMS → 12 Port number → 13 Test calls → 14 Close ticket | Ported number; Confirmation SMS | Customer; Billing team |

What is the MAIN weakness of this SIPOC?`,options:["The Process column is far too detailed — a SIPOC shows only a few high-level steps; the detail belongs on the Level 2 and Level 3 maps","The customer cannot appear as both a supplier and a customer","The Billing team cannot be a customer because it is internal","Outputs must be listed before inputs"],answer:0,hint:"SIPOC = a high-level scoping view of the process.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A SIPOC is a high-level view used to scope the process and identify suppliers, inputs, outputs and customers. Its Process column shows only the **major** steps (typically around four to seven)."},{title:"Apply to the table",body:"Fourteen keystroke-level steps (“Open CRM screen”, “Search customer”) are Level 2/3 detail and hide the big picture."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A customer often supplies inputs (the request and ID) as well as receiving outputs; internal teams are valid customers; and the SIPOC order is fixed in its name."}],final:"Process column too detailed for a SIPOC → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Customers commonly supply inputs (the request, the ID) and also receive outputs — that is correct.","Internal customers are valid customers in a SIPOC.","The layout follows S-I-P-O-C; the column order is not the weakness."],bookRef:"Book p. 111"},{id:"sv-1-236",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:`A CTQ tree reads:

*Reliable home Wi-Fi* → **Stable connection** → *Drop-outs per week ≤ 1 (router log)*

At which level of the tree is **“Stable connection”**?`,options:["Customer need","Driver","CTQ","Specification limit"],answer:1,hint:"Need (general) → drivers (what the need means) → CTQs (measurable, with a spec).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CTQ tree moves from a general **need**, through **drivers** that break the need into its parts, to **CTQs** that are measurable and carry a specification."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"“Stable connection” makes “reliable” more specific but cannot be measured as it stands — it is a **driver**. “Drop-outs per week ≤ 1” is the CTQ with its specification."}],final:"“Stable connection” = driver → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The need is the general statement on the left — “Reliable home Wi-Fi”.","","A CTQ must be measurable with a specification; “Stable connection” has neither.","The specification is the “≤ 1 per week” part of the CTQ on the right."],bookRef:"Book p. 509"},{id:"sv-1-237",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"How does the book define a **Critical to Quality (CTQ)** characteristic?",options:["Any characteristic that is critical to the perceived quality of the product, process or system","Any limit calculated from the process data at ±3 standard deviations","Any input to the process that the team can control","The cheapest way to deliver the service"],answer:0,hint:"CTQ is seen through the customer’s eyes; the glossary links it to the “Significant Y”.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary: a CTQ is **any characteristic critical to the perceived quality** of the product, process or system — see **Significant Y**. It is an output the customer cares about."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"±3σ limits are control limits (voice of the process); controllable inputs are X’s; cost is a business (VOB) concern."}],final:"CTQ = characteristic critical to perceived quality → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","This describes control limits, which come from the process, not from customers.","Controllable inputs are X’s; a CTQ is an output (a Y).","Cost is a business requirement, not a definition of CTQ."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-238",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`The CTQ for home-fibre installation is **≤ 5 working days** from order. Last quarter’s data:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: InstallDays

Variable      N   Mean  StDev  Minimum    Q1  Median    Q3  Maximum
InstallDays 240   4.12   1.63     1.00  3.00    3.90  5.60    11.20
\`\`\`

Which statement is supported by this output?`,options:["Every installation meets the CTQ, because the mean is below 5 days","At least 25% of installations miss the CTQ, because Q3 (5.60) is above 5 days","Only the maximum value (11.20) misses the CTQ","About half of the installations miss the CTQ, because the median is close to 5"],answer:1,hint:"Q3 is the value below which 75% of the data lie; a mean below the limit says nothing about every unit.",steps:[{title:"Read the right number",body:"Q3 = 5.60 days, so 25% of installations took **5.60 days or longer** — all above the 5-day CTQ."},{title:"Interpret",body:"At least a quarter of customers experience a defect, even though the mean (4.12) is inside the CTQ. Customers feel the spread, not the average."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The median (3.90) is below 5, so fewer than half miss — not “about half”. Many values between 5.60 and 11.20 exist, not just the maximum."}],final:"Q3 = 5.60 > 5 → at least 25% miss the CTQ → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A mean inside the specification does not mean every unit is inside it — the spread matters.","","At least 25% of values are 5.60 or higher, so far more than one installation misses the CTQ.","The median is 3.90, so more than half of installations meet the CTQ; “about half missing” is not supported."],trap:"Customers feel the variation, not the average.",bookRef:"Book p. 509"},{id:"sv-1-239",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Survey data show that customer satisfaction rises steadily as **actual broadband speed** rises and falls steadily as it drops — the more, the better. In the **Kano model**, speed is a:",options:["Performance (one-dimensional) requirement","Basic (must-be) requirement","Delighter (attractive) requirement","Indifferent requirement"],answer:0,hint:"Kano: basic = expected; performance = more is better, in proportion; delighter = unexpected bonus.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **performance** (one-dimensional, “more is better”) requirement raises satisfaction in proportion to how well it is delivered, and lowers it when delivered poorly."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Satisfaction moves up and down steadily with speed — the signature of a performance requirement."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A basic need only causes dissatisfaction when missing; a delighter only adds satisfaction when present; an indifferent feature has no effect."}],final:"Linear “more is better” = performance → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A basic need gives no extra satisfaction when delivered well — it only hurts when missing.","A delighter is unexpected; its absence does not lower satisfaction.","An indifferent feature does not move satisfaction at all, but here it moves strongly."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.2"},{id:"sv-1-240",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which CTQ has **only a lower specification limit (LSL)**?",options:["Time to restore a P1 fault ≤ 4 hours","Measured download speed ≥ 90% of the plan speed","Site-survey report issued 60 ± 5 minutes after the visit","Bill error rate ≤ 0.5% per cycle"],answer:1,hint:"“≥” → only a minimum (LSL); “≤” → only a maximum (USL); “±” → both.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A one-sided **LSL** CTQ is written as “at least” (≥): values below the limit are defects, and higher values are fine."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Download speed ≥ 90% of plan has only a minimum."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Restore time and bill error rate are “at most” CTQs (USL only); the site-survey timing has a target with both limits."}],final:"“≥ 90% of plan speed” = LSL only → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Restore time “≤ 4 hours” has only an upper limit (USL).","","A target ± tolerance has both an LSL (55) and a USL (65).","An error rate “≤ 0.5%” has only an upper limit (USL)."],bookRef:"Book p. 112"},{id:"sv-1-241",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"In focus groups, customers say: *“Your call-centre agents are rude.”* Which is the BEST CTQ to derive from this?",options:["Percentage of surveyed calls rated **≥ 4 out of 5 for courtesy**, target **≥ 90%** each month (post-call survey)","Agents must be nicer to customers","Send every agent on a two-day courtesy course","Number of agents employed in the call centre"],answer:0,hint:"A CTQ is a measurable output characteristic with an operational definition and a target or specification.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Turn the vague VOC into something **measurable**: what is measured, how, and the limit that makes a result a defect."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"The survey-rating CTQ has a measure (courtesy rating), a defect definition (< 4), a target (≥ 90%) and a data source."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"“Be nicer” cannot be measured; a training course is a **solution**, not a requirement; headcount is an input, not what customers experience."}],final:"Measurable courtesy rating with a target → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","This only restates the complaint; it has no measure or specification.","This is a solution chosen before any data — a CTQ describes the requirement, not the fix.","Headcount is an input (X), not the courtesy the customer experiences."],bookRef:"Book p. 509"},{id:"sv-1-242",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A draft CTQ reads: *“Install the new auto-routing module so tickets reach the right queue.”* What is the main problem with it?",options:["It has not yet been signed by the Champion","It should be expressed in money rather than in tickets","It is too short to be a CTQ","It states a solution instead of a measurable requirement of the output, such as the % of tickets routed to the right queue first time"],answer:3,hint:"CTQs describe what the customer needs from the output — never how to achieve it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CTQ is a measurable characteristic of the output that the customer cares about. Writing a **solution** into it jumps over Measure and Analyze."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A better CTQ: “% of tickets routed to the correct queue first time ≥ 98% (ticket audit, weekly)”. Whether a new module is the answer is decided later from data."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Sign-off, length and currency are not what makes a CTQ valid."}],final:"A CTQ must not contain a solution → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Champion approval is a charter step; it would not fix a CTQ that is really a solution.","CTQs are measured in the units the customer experiences (here, correct routing), not necessarily in money.","Length is irrelevant; a short CTQ can be well formed.",""],trap:"A solution hidden inside a CTQ or problem statement is a classic Define-phase error.",bookRef:"Book p. 509"},{id:"sv-1-243",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Reading a CTQ tree from left to right, how does the level of detail change?",options:["From specific measures on the left to general needs on the right","From the general customer need, through drivers, to specific measurable CTQs","From causes on the left to effects on the right","From suppliers on the left to customers on the right"],answer:1,hint:"General → specific.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CTQ tree starts with a broad, often vague **need**, breaks it into **drivers**, and ends with **CTQs** that are specific and measurable."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Causes → effect describes a fishbone; suppliers → customers describes a SIPOC."}],final:"Need → drivers → CTQs (general → specific) → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Reversed direction: the tree starts general and ends specific.","","Cause-and-effect logic belongs to the fishbone diagram.","Suppliers-to-customers flow belongs to the SIPOC."],bookRef:"Book p. 509"},{id:"sv-1-244",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A team working on mobile activation has listed **six** CTQs: activation time, first-time activation, correct tariff, welcome SMS sent, SIM delivery time and porting success. The Champion asks what the project should focus on. What should the Black Belt recommend?",options:["Choose the one primary CTQ (Y) most linked to the business problem for this project, and track the others as secondary metrics or later projects","Improve all six CTQs at once so that the project has the biggest impact","Drop CTQs and use customer satisfaction only, because it covers everything","Pick the CTQ with the easiest data to collect, whatever the business problem"],answer:0,hint:"The book advises solving the performance of only one process output (Y) at a time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book warns that trying to optimise more than one Y at a time can become overwhelming, because each Y has its own X’s and detail. Work on **one Y** first; the groundwork then helps with the second and third."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Choose the primary CTQ that links most directly to the problem statement and business case; keep the others visible as secondary metrics."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Six Y’s at once spreads the team too thin; overall satisfaction is too broad to analyse; data convenience is not a reason to pick a Y."}],final:"One primary Y per project → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Six Y’s each have their own X’s — the project becomes unmanageable, against the book’s advice.","Overall satisfaction is a broad need, not a measurable, actionable CTQ.","The Y must be chosen for its link to the business problem, not for convenience."],bookRef:"Book p. 116"},{id:"sv-1-245",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Each business order is checked against three CTQs: **activated on time**, **correct tariff** and **correct billing address**. Order 118 was activated late **and** had the wrong tariff; its address was correct. How is order 118 counted?",options:["1 defect and 1 defective unit","2 defects and 1 defective unit","2 defects and 2 defective units","3 defects and 1 defective unit"],answer:1,hint:"Defect = each failure to meet a requirement; defective = a unit with at least one defect.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary: a **defect** is an output that does not meet a defined specification or requirement; a **defective** is a unit that contains **at least one** defect."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Two CTQs were missed → **2 defects**. They are on one order → **1 defective unit**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A unit is defective once, however many defects it has. The correct address is not a defect, so the count is not 3."}],final:"2 defects, 1 defective → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Counted the order once for both measures; each missed CTQ is a separate defect.","","A unit cannot be defective twice; one order is one defective unit.","Counted the address CTQ, which was met — met requirements are opportunities, not defects."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-246",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`The CTQ for enterprise faults is **restore within 4 hours** (USL = 4). A capability report on last quarter’s **1,250** incidents shows:

\`\`\`
Performance
            Observed   Expected Overall   Expected Within
PPM < LSL          *                  *                 *
PPM > USL   48000.00           52310.44          39876.12
PPM Total   48000.00           52310.44          39876.12
\`\`\`

What percentage of last quarter’s incidents **actually** missed the CTQ?`,options:["0.048%","4.8%","5.23%","3.99%"],answer:1,hint:"Observed PPM counts what really happened; Expected PPM comes from the fitted distribution. Percent = PPM divided by 10,000.",steps:[{title:"Read the right column",body:"“Actually missed” → the **Observed** column: PPM > USL = 48,000."},{title:"Convert to a percentage",body:"$$\\%=\\dfrac{PPM}{10{,}000}=\\dfrac{48{,}000}{10{,}000}=4.8\\%$$"},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Expected Overall (5.23%) and Expected Within (3.99%) are model estimates, not counts of real incidents."}],final:"Observed PPM 48,000 = 4.8% → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Treated the proportion 0.048 as if it were already a percentage — it equals 4.8%.","","Read the Expected Overall column, which is a model estimate, not the observed count.","Read the Expected Within column (short-term model estimate), not what actually happened."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-247",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A provisioning unit’s annual cost of quality is: prevention **OMR 12,000**, appraisal **OMR 28,000**, internal failure **OMR 45,000**, external failure **OMR 75,000**. The company defines **COPQ** as internal plus external failure cost. What percentage of the total cost of quality is COPQ?",options:["28.1%","46.9%","75.0%","92.5%"],answer:2,hint:"COPQ share = $\\dfrac{\\text{internal failure}+\\text{external failure}}{\\text{prevention}+\\text{appraisal}+\\text{internal}+\\text{external}}$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"P = 12,000; A = 28,000; IF = 45,000; EF = 75,000. Total = 160,000."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{COPQ share}=\\dfrac{IF+EF}{P+A+IF+EF}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\text{COPQ share}=\\dfrac{45{,}000+75{,}000}{160{,}000}=\\dfrac{120{,}000}{160{,}000}=0.75=75.0\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Three-quarters of quality spending goes on failures — a strong case for more prevention."}],final:"COPQ = 75.0% of total cost of quality → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Used internal failure only (45,000 ÷ 160,000); external failure is part of COPQ too.","Used external failure only (75,000 ÷ 160,000); internal failure is part of COPQ too.","","Added appraisal to the failure costs (148,000 ÷ 160,000); by the stated definition appraisal is not COPQ."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-248",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which definition of **Cost of Poor Quality (COPQ)** matches the book?",options:["The costs of any activity that is not doing the right thing right the first time","The total budget of the quality department","The cost of buying better-quality materials from suppliers","The cost of training Green Belts and Black Belts"],answer:0,hint:"COPQ = the price of not getting it right first time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary: COPQ is the cost of any activity that is **not doing the right thing right the first time** — the financial measure of waste that is not integral to the product or service."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A quality department’s budget, better materials and Belt training are spending choices (often prevention), not the cost of failing."}],final:"Not right first time = COPQ → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A department budget is not the same as the cost of failures and waste across the process.","Paying for better materials is an investment (prevention), not the cost of poor quality.","Training is a prevention cost, not the cost of doing things wrong."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-249",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"An enterprise customer suffers an outage that breaches its SLA, and the operator pays **service credits**. In which cost-of-quality category do these credits belong?",options:["Prevention","Appraisal","Internal failure","External failure"],answer:3,hint:"Internal failure = found before the customer; external failure = the customer is affected.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Failure costs are split by **who finds the failure**: internal failure is found before the output reaches the customer; **external failure** reaches the customer."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The customer suffered the outage and was compensated → **external failure**."}],final:"SLA credits after a customer-facing failure = external failure → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Prevention costs are spent to stop failures happening (planning, training, error-proofing).","Appraisal costs are spent on checking and inspecting.","Internal failure is caught before the customer is affected; here the customer felt the outage.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.3"},{id:"sv-1-250",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A junior analyst classified one year of billing quality costs:

\`\`\`
Row  Cost item                                        Category       OMR
1    Supplier certification audit before contract     Prevention    9,000
2    Testing each bill run before release             Appraisal    24,000
3    Re-running failed bill batches before dispatch   Internal F   31,000
4    Credits after customers report overcharging      Internal F   47,000
5    Staff training on new tariff rules               Prevention   12,000
\`\`\`

Which row is **misclassified**?`,options:["Row 4 — it is an external failure cost","Row 1 — it is an appraisal cost","Row 3 — it is an appraisal cost","Row 5 — it is an internal failure cost"],answer:0,hint:"Ask for each row: prevent, check, fail before the customer, or fail at the customer?",steps:[{title:"Classify each row",body:"Row 1: qualifying a supplier **before** work starts prevents problems → prevention ✓. Row 2: testing → appraisal ✓. Row 3: failure caught before dispatch → internal failure ✓. Row 5: training → prevention ✓."},{title:"Find the error",body:"Row 4: customers found the overcharge and were credited → **external** failure, not internal."}],final:"Row 4 should be external failure → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Certifying a supplier before the contract stops problems at source — prevention is correct.","Re-running failed batches is fixing a failure found before the customer — internal failure is correct.","Training stops errors happening — prevention is correct."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.3"},{id:"sv-1-251",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A care unit compares its cost of quality over two years:

\`\`\`
Tabulated Statistics: Category, Year; Sum of Cost (OMR 000)

Category            2025   2026
Prevention            10     35
Appraisal             30     40
Internal failure      80     55
External failure     120     60
All                  240    190
\`\`\`

Which interpretation is BEST supported?`,options:["Quality got worse, because prevention and appraisal spending went up","Spending 35 thousand more on prevention and appraisal went with 85 thousand less failure cost, so the total cost of quality fell by 50 thousand","COPQ rose, because prevention costs are part of failure costs","The total cost of quality fell by 85 thousand"],answer:1,hint:"Compare (prevention + appraisal) with (internal + external failure) in each year.",steps:[{title:"Read the changes",body:"P + A: 40 → 75 (+35). Failure: 200 → 115 (−85). Total: 240 → 190 (−50)."},{title:"Interpret",body:"Investing in prevention and appraisal went together with a much larger drop in failure costs — the classic cost-of-quality pattern."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Higher prevention spend is not worse quality; prevention is not a failure cost; −85 is the failure-cost change, not the total change."}],final:"+35 P/A, −85 failure, −50 total → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Higher prevention and appraisal spend is an investment; the failure costs show quality improved.","","Prevention is not part of COPQ (failure costs); failure costs actually fell from 200 to 115.","Confused the fall in failure costs (−85) with the fall in total cost of quality (−50)."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-252",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are **appraisal** costs EXCEPT:",options:["Re-doing orders that failed provisioning because of wrong data","Inspecting incoming routers from the supplier","Auditing each bill run before release","Calibrating the line-test equipment"],answer:0,hint:"Appraisal = measuring, checking, inspecting, auditing.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Appraisal costs pay for **checking** whether work meets requirements: inspection, testing, audits, calibration of test equipment."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"Re-doing failed orders is **rework** after a failure found inside the company → **internal failure**."}],final:"Rework is internal failure, not appraisal → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incoming inspection is a checking activity — appraisal.","A pre-release audit is a checking activity — appraisal.","Calibrating test equipment keeps checks accurate — appraisal."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.3"},{id:"sv-1-253",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Finance says the provisioning team has “no quality cost problem” because the profit-and-loss report has no line for rework. Process data show that **18%** of orders are re-keyed by staff. What is the BEST response from the Black Belt?",options:["Agree, because only costs reported by Finance are real costs","Explain that this rework is a hidden factory: it is absorbed in normal salaries and overtime, so it must be estimated from process data","Classify the re-keying as prevention, because it stops wrong orders reaching customers","Ignore the re-keying, because staff are paid anyway"],answer:1,hint:"The hidden factory is corrective, non-value-added work that is generally not recognised as waste.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary defines the **hidden factory** as corrective and non-value-added work that is **generally not recognised** as a generator of waste in resources, materials and cost."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Re-keying is paid through ordinary salaries and overtime, so it never shows as its own line. The Black Belt estimates it: volume × rework rate × time × loaded cost."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Rework is internal failure, not prevention; and “staff are paid anyway” ignores the capacity being wasted."}],final:"Re-keying = hidden factory, estimate it from data → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Most COPQ is not reported as its own line — that is exactly why it is called hidden.","","Rework after an error is internal failure; prevention stops the error happening in the first place.","Paid time spent on rework is capacity that could serve customers — it is a real cost."],bookRef:"Book p. 586"},{id:"sv-1-254",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"COPQ is often drawn as an **iceberg**. Which of these costs sits **below the waterline** — real, but hardest to see in the accounts?",options:["Service credits paid under SLA terms","Replacement of faulty routers under warranty","Scrapped SIM cards with printing errors","Future revenue lost from customers who quietly leave after a poor experience"],answer:3,hint:"Visible costs have an invoice or a scrap note; hidden costs have none.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Above the waterline are costs that appear in records — credits, warranty, scrap. Below it are larger, hidden costs: lost customers, lost goodwill, extra capacity, expediting, management time."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Customers who leave without complaining create no record at all, yet the lost revenue can be the largest item."}],final:"Silent churn = hidden COPQ → answer **D**",whyWrong:["SLA credits are recorded payments — a visible cost above the waterline.","Warranty replacements are recorded — a visible cost.","Scrap is counted and written off — a visible cost.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 586"},{id:"sv-1-255",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt lists these annual costs:

| # | Cost item |
|---|---|
| 1 | Re-provisioning orders that failed internal line tests |
| 2 | Engineer time to correct billing errors found by the pre-release audit |
| 3 | The pre-release bill audit itself |
| 4 | Refunds to customers for overbilling |
| 5 | SIM cards scrapped for print errors before dispatch |
| 6 | Training staff on the new tariff system |

How many of these are **internal failure** costs?`,options:["2","3","4","5"],answer:1,hint:"Internal failure = fixing or scrapping something that failed, before the customer receives it.",steps:[{title:"Classify each row",body:"1 internal failure; 2 internal failure (fixing errors that the audit found); 3 appraisal; 4 external failure; 5 internal failure; 6 prevention."},{title:"Count",body:"Rows 1, 2 and 5 → **3** internal failure costs."},{title:"Watch the trap",body:"The **audit** (row 3) is appraisal, but **fixing what it finds** (row 2) is internal failure."}],final:"3 internal failure costs → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Treated row 2 as appraisal; correcting the errors an audit finds is internal failure, not checking.","","Added row 4 (customer refunds), which is external failure because the customer was affected.","Counted every cost except training — appraisal and external failure were wrongly included."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.3"},{id:"sv-1-256",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which description fits a **Pareto chart**?",options:["A line chart of one measure plotted in time order","A histogram showing the shape of continuous data","A bar chart of categorical (attribute) data, with the categories in descending order of frequency, usually with a cumulative-percentage line","A plot of one variable against another to look for a relationship"],answer:2,hint:"Categories, sorted largest to smallest.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary: a Pareto chart is a **bar chart for attribute (categorical) data** with the categories presented in **descending order of frequency**. A cumulative-percentage line is normally added."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A line chart over time is a run chart; a histogram shows the distribution of continuous data; one variable against another is a scatter plot."}],final:"Sorted bars of categories = Pareto chart → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This describes a run chart, which shows performance over time.","A histogram bins continuous data in numeric order, not categories sorted by size.","","This describes a scatter plot."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-257",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A care centre has nine complaint categories. Handling one complaint costs between **OMR 2** and **OMR 150**, depending on the category. The project goal is to cut the **cost of poor quality**. How should the Pareto chart be built?",options:["By complaint count only, because frequency is always the right measure","In alphabetical order, so the categories are easy to find","By the number of staff working on each category","By total cost per category (count × cost per complaint), sorted from largest to smallest"],answer:3,hint:"The bar height should be the measure that matches the project goal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Pareto chart can be weighted by **count, cost, time or impact**. When the cost per item varies a lot, a count Pareto can point to a frequent but cheap category."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The goal is COPQ, and the cost per complaint varies 75-fold, so the bars should show **total cost** per category."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Alphabetical order is not a Pareto chart; staff numbers are an input, not the impact of the problem."}],final:"Weight the Pareto by cost → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Frequency is not always the right measure; with very different unit costs it can point to a cheap category.","A Pareto chart is always sorted largest to smallest, never alphabetically.","Staff numbers measure effort allocated, not the cost of the quality problem.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-258",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`A team charts **500** repeat broadband faults:

\`\`\`
Pareto Chart of Fault Reason

Reason    NoSync  WrongCPE  Wiring  Config  Other
Count        220       130      70      45     35
Percent     44.0      26.0    14.0     9.0    7.0
Cum %       44.0      70.0    84.0    93.0  100.0
\`\`\`

Using the 80:20 rule as a guide, which categories are the **vital few**?`,options:["NoSync only","NoSync and WrongCPE","NoSync, WrongCPE and Wiring","All categories except Other"],answer:2,hint:"Read the Cum % row and stop at the first category where it reaches about 80%.",steps:[{title:"Read the right row",body:"Cum %: 44.0 → 70.0 → **84.0** → 93.0 → 100.0."},{title:"Decide",body:"The first three categories (3 of 5 reasons) reach 84% — the first point at or above about 80%. They are the vital few."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Two categories cover only 70%; including Config adds just 9 more points and dilutes the focus."}],final:"Cum % reaches 84% after three bars → answer **C**",whyWrong:["NoSync alone covers only 44% of faults.","Stopped at 70% cumulative — below the 80% guide.","","Including Config (93%) goes well past the 80% point and adds a trivial-many category."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-259",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A service desk draws two Pareto charts for one month of tickets:

\`\`\`
Pareto Chart of Ticket Type (count)
Type       PwdReset  SlowSpeed  WrongPlan  DataOutage
Count           300        180        120          40
Percent        46.9       28.1       18.8         6.3
Cum %          46.9       75.0       93.8       100.0

Pareto Chart of Ticket Type (cost, OMR)
Type       DataOutage  WrongPlan  SlowSpeed  PwdReset
Cost             6000       1200        900       600
Percent          69.0       13.8       10.3        6.9
Cum %            69.0       82.8       93.1      100.0
\`\`\`

The project goal is to reduce the **cost of poor quality**. Which ticket type should the team tackle **first**?`,options:["PwdReset","SlowSpeed","WrongPlan","DataOutage"],answer:3,hint:"Pick the chart whose measure matches the goal, then take its first bar.",steps:[{title:"Match the chart to the goal",body:"The goal is cost, so the **cost** Pareto is the one to read."},{title:"Read it",body:"DataOutage = OMR 6,000 = **69.0%** of the cost, although it is only 6.3% of the tickets."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"PwdReset is the tallest **count** bar but only 6.9% of the cost. WrongPlan is second on cost (13.8%)."}],final:"DataOutage = 69% of cost → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Read the count Pareto: password resets are frequent but cheap (6.9% of cost).","SlowSpeed is second by count and only third by cost (10.3%).","WrongPlan is second on the cost chart (13.8%), well behind DataOutage.",""],trap:"The tallest count bar is not always the biggest problem.",bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-260",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Before the project, “Billing error” was the tallest bar on the complaint Pareto. After the improvement, a new Pareto for the same length of time shows “Wrong plan” as the tallest bar. A manager says: *“The project failed — there is still a big top bar.”* What is the BEST response?",options:["Agree, and restart the project on the same category","Remove the “Wrong plan” category so that the chart looks flatter","Compare the before and after charts on the same scale and time period — there will always be a tallest bar; success is shown by the drop in total and in the Billing error bar","Draw the after chart over a longer period so that it looks the same as the before chart"],answer:2,hint:"Pareto charts compare categories within one chart; improvement is judged by comparing counts across charts on a common scale.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Every Pareto chart has a tallest bar by construction. To show improvement, draw before and after charts for **equal time periods** on the **same vertical scale** and compare the totals and the targeted bar."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"If Billing errors fell sharply and the total fell, the project worked; “Wrong plan” is simply the new top bar and could become the next project."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Deleting a category or changing the period hides the facts instead of comparing like with like."}],final:"Compare like with like; a top bar always exists → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A new top bar does not mean failure — any Pareto chart has one.","Removing a real category distorts the data.","","Different time periods make the comparison unfair — before and after must cover the same length of time."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-261",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are true of a Pareto chart EXCEPT:",options:["The bars are ordered from largest to smallest","A cumulative-percentage line is often added","The vertical axis can show count, cost or another weighting","It shows the root cause of the largest category"],answer:3,hint:"Pareto answers “which category?”, not “why?”.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Pareto chart ranks categories so the team can focus on the vital few. It shows **where** the problem is concentrated, not **why** it happens."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"Root causes are found later with tools such as the fishbone, 5 Whys and hypothesis tests."}],final:"Pareto does not show root causes → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Descending order is the defining feature of a Pareto chart.","A cumulative line is a standard part of a Pareto chart.","Weighting by cost, time or impact is allowed and often better than count.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-262",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"The Pareto principle applies only when **exactly** 20% of the causes produce **exactly** 80% of the effect. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"The 80:20 rule is a rule of thumb, not a fixed law.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary states the Pareto principle as: the majority of influence on an outcome is exerted by a **minority** of input factors. 80:20 is a guide; real splits may be 70:30, 90:10 or 75:15."},{title:"Decide",body:"The word “exactly” makes the statement **False**."}],final:"80:20 is a guide, not an exact law → answer **B** (False)",whyWrong:["The principle says a minority of causes drives the majority of the effect; the exact split varies from case to case.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-263",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A Pareto chart of **1,000** activation failures shows that **“SIM not provisioned”** is 46% of the total. The team now wants to brainstorm **why** SIMs are not provisioned. Which tool should it use next?",options:["Control chart","Histogram","Cause-and-effect (fishbone) diagram","SIPOC"],answer:2,hint:"Pareto = which; fishbone = why.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Pareto chart has done its job — it chose the vital category. To list possible **causes** of that category, the team uses a **cause-and-effect (fishbone)** diagram."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A control chart checks stability over time; a histogram shows the shape of continuous data; a SIPOC scopes the process."}],final:"From “which” to “why” → fishbone → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A control chart shows stability over time, not possible causes of a failure type.","A histogram shows the distribution of continuous data; failure categories are attribute data.","","A SIPOC scopes the process at a high level; it does not explore causes."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-264",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt charts **380** complaints. The “Other” bar combines **11** small categories:

\`\`\`
Pareto Chart of Complaint Category

Category   Billing  Network  Delivery  Other
Count           96       74        60    150
Percent       25.3     19.5      15.8   39.5
Cum %         25.3     44.7      60.5  100.0
\`\`\`

What is the BEST next step?`,options:["Start work on Billing straight away, because it is the tallest named bar","Move “Other” to the first position, because it is the largest","Conclude that the Pareto principle does not apply and stop using the data","Review the category definitions and break down “Other”, because a very large “Other” bar hides the real vital few"],answer:3,hint:"“Other” should be small; when it is the biggest bar, the categories need rework.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"“Other” holds 39.5% of complaints — more than any named category."},{title:"Interpret",body:"When “Other” is this large, the categories are too narrow or poorly defined. The real vital few may be hidden inside it, so the team should re-code or split “Other” before choosing a focus."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"“Other” always stays at the far right, whatever its size. Jumping to Billing ignores 40% of the data; one badly coded chart does not disprove the Pareto principle."}],final:"Break down “Other” first → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Billing is only 25.3%; choosing it now ignores the 39.5% hidden in “Other”.","By convention “Other” is always placed last, however large it is.","The pattern points to poor categories, not to a failure of the Pareto principle.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-265",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A planning team audits **250** site-survey reports. It finds **40** defects, which sit on **32** of the reports. What is the **DPU**?",options:["0.128","0.16","0.84","1.25"],answer:1,hint:"$DPU=\\dfrac{\\text{total defects}}{\\text{total units}}$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Units = 250 reports; defects = 40 (on 32 defective reports)."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$DPU=\\dfrac{\\text{defects}}{\\text{units}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$DPU=\\dfrac{40}{250}=0.16$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"On average each report carries 0.16 defects. The 32 defective reports are used for % defective (32 out of 250 = 12.8%), not DPU."}],final:"DPU = 0.16 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Used defective reports (32) instead of defects (40): this is the proportion defective, not DPU.","","Took 1 − 0.16, treating DPU as if it were a yield.","Divided defects by defective reports (40 out of 32): defects per defective unit, not per unit."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-266",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A three-step porting process has first time yields of **0.99**, **0.94** and **0.97**. What is the **rolled throughput yield (RTY)**, to two decimals?",options:["0.97","0.94","0.90","0.10"],answer:2,hint:"RTY = product of the step first time yields.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"FTY₁ = 0.99, FTY₂ = 0.94, FTY₃ = 0.97."},{title:"Pick the formula and substitute",body:"$$RTY=FTY_1\\times FTY_2\\times FTY_3=0.99\\times 0.94\\times 0.97=0.9027$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"RTY ≈ **0.90**: about 90% of requests pass all three steps with no defect and no rework."}],final:"RTY = 0.90 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Averaged the step yields (0.967) instead of multiplying them.","Used only the lowest step yield; RTY must include every step.","","Reported 1 − RTY (the share that needs rework somewhere), not RTY."],bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-267",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A router-repair depot works one **8-hour** shift with **30 minutes** of breaks. Customers need **225** repaired routers a day. What is the **takt time**?",options:["6.40 min","2.13 min","2.00 min","0.50 min"],answer:2,hint:"$\\text{Takt}=\\dfrac{\\text{net available working time}}{\\text{customer demand}}$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Shift = 480 min; breaks = 30 min; net time = 450 min; demand = 225 units a day."},{title:"Pick the formula and substitute",body:"$$\\text{Takt}=\\dfrac{\\text{net available time}}{\\text{demand}}=\\dfrac{480-30}{225}=\\dfrac{450}{225}=2.00\\text{ min}$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"One repaired router must be finished every **2 minutes** to keep pace with demand."}],final:"Takt = 2.00 min → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Used the whole 24-hour day (1,440 min) instead of the working time available.","Forgot to subtract the 30 minutes of breaks (480 out of 225).","","Inverted the formula (demand divided by time): 0.5 units per minute is a rate, not a takt time."],bookRef:"Book p. 105"},{id:"sv-1-268",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A roaming team checks **500** activation requests. Each request has **4** defect opportunities. The check finds **30** defects, on **24** of the requests. What is the **DPMO**?",options:["3,750","12,000","15,000","60,000"],answer:2,hint:"$DPMO=\\dfrac{\\text{defects}}{\\text{units}\\times\\text{opportunities per unit}}\\times 10^6$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Units = 500; opportunities per unit = 4; defects = 30."},{title:"Pick the formula and substitute",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{\\text{defects}}{\\text{units}\\times\\text{opps}}\\times 10^6=\\dfrac{30}{500\\times 4}\\times 10^6=\\dfrac{30}{2{,}000}\\times 10^6=15{,}000$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"DPMO = **15,000**. The 24 defective requests are not used — DPMO counts defects."}],final:"DPMO = 15,000 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Divided by the opportunities twice (30 out of 8,000), counting 4 × 4 per unit.","Used the 24 defective requests instead of the 30 defects.","","Left out the opportunities per unit (30 out of 500) — that is DPU × 10⁶, not DPMO."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-269",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A SIM-dispatch line has **7.5 hours** of net working time a day and customer demand of **300** orders a day. Cycle times for each step:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Cycle time (s)

Step        N    Mean   StDev
Receive    50    62.4     8.1
Verify     50    84.0    10.6
Configure  50   104.2    12.7
Dispatch   50    71.3     9.4
\`\`\`

Which step **cannot keep up** with customer demand?`,options:["Receive","Verify","Dispatch","Configure"],answer:3,hint:"Compare each step’s cycle time with $\\text{Takt}=\\dfrac{\\text{net available time}}{\\text{demand}}$; a step slower than takt cannot keep up.",steps:[{title:"Find takt time",body:"$$\\text{Takt}=\\dfrac{7.5\\times 3{,}600}{300}=\\dfrac{27{,}000}{300}=90\\text{ s}$$"},{title:"Compare",body:"A step whose mean cycle time is **above** takt cannot meet demand. Configure = 104.2 s > 90 s. Verify (84.0 s) is close but still inside takt."},{title:"Decide",body:"Configure is the bottleneck."}],final:"Configure 104.2 s > takt 90 s → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Receive (62.4 s) is well inside the 90-second takt.","Verify (84.0 s) is the closest to takt but is still below 90 s.","Dispatch (71.3 s) is inside the 90-second takt.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 105"},{id:"sv-1-270",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team counts how many times each business order had to be **reworked** inside one process step:

\`\`\`
Tally for Discrete Variables: ReworkLoops

ReworkLoops  Count  Percent
0              342    85.50
1               41    10.25
2               12     3.00
3                5     1.25
N=             400
\`\`\`

What is the **first time yield (FTY)** of this step?`,options:["14.5%","20.0%","95.75%","85.5%"],answer:3,hint:"$FTY=\\dfrac{\\text{units passing with no rework}}{\\text{units entering}}$",steps:[{title:"Read the right row",body:"Orders with **0** rework loops passed first time: 342 out of 400."},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$FTY=\\dfrac{342}{400}=0.855=85.5\\%$$"},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"An order reworked once did **not** pass first time, so it is not part of FTY."}],final:"FTY = 85.5% → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This is the share of orders that needed rework (1 − FTY).","This is the average number of rework loops per order (80 loops out of 400 orders), not a yield.","Counted orders reworked once as passing first time (342 + 41).",""],bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-271",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`An auditor compares two processes:

\`\`\`
Audit summary

Process        Units  Opps/unit  Defects    DPU    DPMO
Billing         2000         20      160  0.080   4,000
Provisioning    1000          4       60  0.060  15,000
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct when the **complexity** of the two outputs is taken into account?`,options:["Billing performs better: its DPU is higher, but each bill has five times as many opportunities, so its DPMO is much lower","Provisioning performs better, because its DPU is lower","The two processes perform the same, because their defect counts are similar","Processes with different units can never be compared"],answer:0,hint:"DPMO normalises for the number of opportunities, so it compares outputs of different complexity.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"DPU ignores how complex a unit is. **DPMO** divides by opportunities, so it is the fair basis for comparing processes whose units differ in complexity."},{title:"Read the output",body:"Billing DPMO = 4,000; Provisioning DPMO = 15,000. Billing has fewer defects per opportunity, even though its DPU is higher."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Ranking by DPU penalises the more complex bill. Raw defect counts ignore volume and complexity. Comparing unlike processes is exactly what DPMO is for."}],final:"Lower DPMO = better → Billing → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Compared DPU, which ignores that a bill has 20 opportunities against 4 for an order.","Raw defect counts ignore both the number of units and the opportunities per unit.","DPMO is designed to compare outputs of different complexity."],trap:"Different complexity → compare DPMO, not DPU.",bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-272",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"The activation manager reports a **97% yield**, counting every order that failed a step but was fixed and then passed. Which metric would expose the **rework hidden** in this figure?",options:["Final (end-of-line) yield","The number of orders completed","Average lead time per order","First time yield at each step, rolled up into RTY"],answer:3,hint:"FTY and RTY count only units that pass with no defect and no rework.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A yield that counts reworked units as good hides the **hidden factory**. **FTY** counts only units right the first time, and **RTY** — the product of the step FTYs — is the probability of passing every step with zero defects."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"If the steps have FTYs of, say, 0.90, 0.92 and 0.95, the RTY is about 0.79, even though 97% finally pass."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Final yield is what the manager already reports; output counts ignore quality; lead time may rise with rework but does not measure it directly."}],final:"FTY / RTY reveal the hidden rework → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Final yield counts reworked units as good — it is the figure that hides the rework.","A count of completed orders says nothing about how many needed rework.","Lead time may grow with rework, but it does not count or reveal the rework.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-273",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"To report a better DPMO, a team changes the number of defect opportunities on each bill from **8** to **40** by counting every printed field, including fields that never fail. Defects and bill volumes stay the same. What is the effect?",options:["DPU falls in the same proportion as DPMO","DPMO rises, because there are more opportunities to fail","There is no effect, because DPMO does not depend on the number of opportunities","DPMO falls by a factor of five without any real improvement — opportunities should be only characteristics that matter and can realistically fail, and they should stay constant"],answer:3,hint:"Opportunities sit in the denominator of DPMO but not of DPU.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary defines opportunities as characteristics that can be classified as acceptable or unacceptable. Counting trivial ones inflates the denominator and **flatters** DPMO."},{title:"Illustrate",body:`For 1,000 bills with 40 defects:
$$DPMO_8=\\dfrac{40}{1{,}000\\times 8}\\times 10^6=5{,}000$$
$$DPMO_{40}=\\dfrac{40}{1{,}000\\times 40}\\times 10^6=1{,}000$$
Same defects, five times “better” DPMO.`},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"DPU (defects per unit) does not use opportunities, so it does not change. DPMO falls, it does not rise."}],final:"More (trivial) opportunities → DPMO falls falsely → answer **D**",whyWrong:["DPU does not use opportunities, so it stays the same.","Reversed: more opportunities in the denominator makes DPMO smaller, not larger.","DPMO divides by units × opportunities, so it depends directly on the opportunity count.",""],trap:"Inflating opportunities is a way to game DPMO — fix the definition and keep it constant.",bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-274",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement correctly distinguishes **takt time** from **cycle time**?",options:["Takt time is set by customer demand (available time divided by demand); cycle time is how long the process actually takes to complete a unit","Takt time is how long the process actually takes; cycle time is set by customer demand","They are two names for the same measure","Takt time includes waiting time between steps; cycle time does not include any time at all for queues"],answer:0,hint:"Takt = the customer’s pace; cycle time = the process’s pace.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"$$\\text{Takt}=\\dfrac{\\text{net available time}}{\\text{customer demand}}$$ It is the **pace the customer requires**. **Cycle time** is what the process actually achieves for a task or unit."},{title:"Apply",body:"If cycle time is longer than takt, the process cannot keep up with demand."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The second option swaps the two terms; they are not the same; takt is a demand rate, not a measure of waiting."}],final:"Takt = demand pace, cycle time = actual pace → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Swapped the two definitions.","Takt is a target pace from demand; cycle time is measured from the process — they are different.","Takt is calculated from demand and available time; it is not a measure of waiting."],bookRef:"Book pp. 105, 585"},{id:"sv-1-275",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Each of the following would normally **raise the RTY** of an order process EXCEPT:",options:["Error-proofing the address-entry step","Removing an unnecessary approval step that creates its own defects","Improving the first time yield of the credit check","Adding a final inspection that catches defective orders and sends them back for rework"],answer:3,hint:"RTY is the product of first time yields; rework does not make a unit “right first time”.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"$$RTY=FTY_1\\times FTY_2\\times\\cdots\\times FTY_n$$ RTY rises only if a step’s FTY rises or a defect-producing step is removed."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"A final inspection with rework may improve what the customer receives, but the units still failed first time — every FTY stays the same, and the inspection adds cost (the hidden factory)."}],final:"Inspection + rework does not raise RTY → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Error-proofing raises that step’s FTY, so RTY rises.","Removing a step that creates defects removes a factor below 1, so RTY rises.","A higher FTY at any step raises RTY.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-276",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"The RTY of a process can never be **higher** than the first time yield of its worst step. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Multiplying by numbers between 0 and 1 can only keep a value the same or make it smaller.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"RTY is the product of all step FTYs. Each FTY is at most 1, so multiplying the worst FTY by the others can only keep it the same or reduce it."},{title:"Decide",body:"RTY ≤ the lowest step FTY, so the statement is **True**."}],final:"RTY ≤ min(FTY) → answer **A** (True)",whyWrong:["","Because every other factor is at most 1, the product can never exceed the smallest step FTY — the statement is correct."],bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-277",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:`In which order should a team work to calculate **DPMO** for a contract-review process?

- **P** Count the defects found
- **Q** Define the unit (for example, one contract)
- **R** Define the defect opportunities per unit
- **S** Divide defects by units × opportunities and multiply by one million`,options:["P → Q → R → S","Q → P → R → S","Q → R → P → S","R → Q → P → S"],answer:2,hint:"You cannot count defects consistently until the unit and its opportunities are defined.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Define what a **unit** is, then what counts as an **opportunity** on it (this also defines what a defect is), then **count** defects, then **calculate**."},{title:"Order them",body:"Q → R → P → S."}],final:"Unit → opportunities → count → calculate → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Counting defects before the unit and opportunities are defined gives inconsistent counts.","Counted defects before defining the opportunities, so the team does not yet know what to check.","","Opportunities are defined per unit, so the unit must be defined first."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-278",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"How does the book define **rolled throughput yield (RTY)**?",options:["The share of units that pass final inspection after any rework","The average of the first time yields of all steps","The probability that a unit goes through all process steps with zero defects","Total defects divided by total units"],answer:2,hint:"RTY looks at the whole chain of steps, right first time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary: RTY is the **probability of a unit going through all process steps with zero defects**. It is calculated by multiplying the step FTYs."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Passing after rework is final yield; averaging step yields overstates performance; defects divided by units is DPU."}],final:"RTY = P(zero defects through every step) → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This is final yield, which counts reworked units as good.","Averaging step yields overstates performance; RTY multiplies them.","","This is the definition of DPU."],bookRef:"Book p. 588"}];export{e as items};
